aint cannot be breached.\"}],\"satisfies_preferred\":true,\"satisfies_fallback\":true,\"minimal\":true,\"preserves_intent\":true,\"reasons\":[\"Preferred level is complete: the single preferred element is carried by operative language, not by the comment — new 4.2(a) states affirmatively that no termination payment, liquidated damages or penalty of any kind is payable by the Company, and it reaches every termination scenario, including our own termination for HDI's default.\",\"Fallback is satisfied a fortiori (zero liability is within a three-months'-fees cap and is necessarily the 'sole' amount), so the redline is position-complete at both levels.\",\"Clean-up recommended (residual risk, not an element failure): the defined term \\\"Termination Payment\\\" lived in the deleted 4.2(a), so retained 4.2(b) and (c) now reference an undefined term, and (c) still reads \\\"The Termination Payment, if any, shall be payable by the Company in eight (8) equal payments.\\\" HDI could use that as a textual hook to argue a residual obligation. Repair by striking 4.2(b) and (c) outright or replacing them with \\\"(b)-(c) Intentionally omitted.\\\"; (a) is categorical and (c) is conditional, so the position holds either way, but the tidier version removes the argument.\",\"Minimality holds: only 4.2(a) is rewritten. Section 4.1 (one-year term, auto-renewal, 90 days' notice, Termination Date), Section 3.1 fee mechanics, and the favourable Section 1.6 \\\"without penalty\\\" carve-out are untouched, and no new obligation outside the LD checklist (no notice, audit or credit mechanics) is bolted on.\",\"Intent preserved: the prohibition is limited to \\\"termination payment, liquidated damages or penalty,\\\" so fees accrued under Section 3.1 before the Termination Date are unaffected and no allocation, timing or remedy elsewhere is contradicted. If HDI resists on that ground, a one-line clarifier (\\\"for the avoidance of doubt, this Section does not affect fees accrued prior to the Termination Date\\\") is a low-cost concession that keeps the element intact.\",\"Margin comment is on-tone and playbook-consistent: it quantifies the exposure (50% of trailing Net Sales over $800,000 versus roughly 7.5% of annual Net Sales for three months' fees), states the preferred position, and offers the capped early-termination fee fallback without lecturing.\"],\"severityAdjustment\":\"critical\"}"
